WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.053 --> 00:00:01.730
Just over 50 years ago,

00:00:01.730 --> 00:00:03.700
the Austrian government decided to build

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:06.970
three nuclear reactors
along the Danube River.

00:00:06.970 --> 00:00:09.370
It was the age of the atom,
the height of the space race.

00:00:09.370 --> 00:00:13.570
Nuclear power seemed like the
natural next step for Austria.

00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:15.000
But politically, it was risky,

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:17.040
and the decision was
extremely controversial.

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:19.820
It took many years of
arguments and protests.

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:22.260
But eventually, the country
held a referendum.

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:24.117
The government asked
the people of Austria:

00:00:24.117 --> 00:00:25.940
"do you want nuclear power?"

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:27.480
And the vote was on a knife-edge.

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:31.340
When everything was counted,
50.5% said...

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:32.540
"No".

00:00:33.610 --> 00:00:36.850
The trouble was, they'd already
built the first reactor,

00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:39.230
they just hadn't turned it on yet.

00:00:39.230 --> 00:00:45.380
- This nuclear power plant was
built up from 1972 to 1976.

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:47.880
Everything was here.

00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:53.410
The power plant was ready for operation,
including the fuel rods.

00:00:53.410 --> 00:00:58.810
They only have to push the red button
to put it into operation.

00:00:58.810 --> 00:01:03.780
And then, the chancellor of Austria
decided to hold a public referendum.

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:07.720
We like to say this
special nuclear power plant

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:10.310
never splits any atoms,

00:01:10.310 --> 00:01:14.290
but people, families
and political parties.

00:01:14.290 --> 00:01:17.800
The capacity of the nuclear
power plant in Zwentendorf

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:21.000
was 750 megawatts.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:24.700
This reactor should have
produced electricity

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:27.470
for 1.8 million households.

00:01:27.470 --> 00:01:31.770
- So what do you do with a
never-used nuclear power plant?

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:34.360
It's not like you can just
flat-pack it and ship it over the border

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:36.280
to somewhere that does want it.

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:38.390
And this was incredibly expensive.

00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:44.290
Adjusting for inflation, in today's money,
this plant cost over a billion euro.

00:01:44.290 --> 00:01:48.530
- When the result of the public referendum

00:01:48.530 --> 00:01:51.990
was mentioned on the national TV stations,

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:56.370
for 200 people here in
Zwentendorf it was a shock.

00:01:56.370 --> 00:02:00.040
Nobody estimated this result.

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:04.930
The owners, they decided
to conservate the reactor

00:02:04.930 --> 00:02:08.900
because they thought that politicians
will change their mind

00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:12.800
and they are able to put
the reactor into operation

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:14.820
in one, or two, or three years.

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:16.230
But that never happened.

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:21.320
So in 1985, they started to
sell parts of the equipment.

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:25.100
But this selling process
was not very successful,

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:30.720
and today we have 99% of
the equipment still here.

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:36.240
EVN bought the nuclear
power plant in 2005.

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:40.120
We don't have plans now to use it.

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:44.710
But if there's some day the need
for a gas-fired power plant

00:02:44.710 --> 00:02:48.820
or an alternative-fired power plant,
for example with biomass,

00:02:48.820 --> 00:02:50.340
it's a perfect location.

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:55.500
In the meantime, it's a training centre
for nuclear engineers

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:59.410
for training how you can
rebuild a reactor.

00:02:59.410 --> 00:03:02.190
We offer it as a filming location.

00:03:02.190 --> 00:03:08.240
We use it for music festivals,
15,000 people outside and inside.

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:12.790
We installed, in 2009,
a solar power plant.

00:03:12.790 --> 00:03:16.600
And since 2010, we offer guided tours.

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:20.590
It's open to the public.
You can book your reservation,

00:03:20.590 --> 00:03:25.530
but normally we are full for
six months within some minutes.

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:30.400
EVN bought not only a technical building,

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:37.150
we bought also a very specific piece
of Austria's contemporary history.

00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:42.090
Here, everything is like it
was more than 40 years ago.

00:03:42.090 --> 00:03:45.130
I love the special atmosphere
you have here,

00:03:45.130 --> 00:03:51.220
and I'm really glad to
present it to other people.

00:03:51.220 --> 00:03:53.300
- After the referendum, Austria went on

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:56.680
to entirely ban nuclear
power within its borders,

00:03:56.680 --> 00:03:59.330
maybe because the residents
had concerns about accidents,

00:03:59.330 --> 00:04:02.990
or maybe because they didn't want to deal
with the waste and long-term planning,

00:04:02.990 --> 00:04:05.480
or maybe, if you believe
conspiracy theories,

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:08.730
because some of the people
who ran energy companies back then

00:04:08.730 --> 00:04:10.740
were worried about prices dropping

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:13.510
and threw a load of money
at a scare campaign.

00:04:13.510 --> 00:04:16.970
The story changes a lot
depending on who's telling it.

00:04:16.970 --> 00:04:20.950
And it is the politically easy option
to phase out nuclear power.

00:04:20.950 --> 00:04:24.610
Particularly now as wind and solar
continue to become more and more viable,

00:04:24.610 --> 00:04:27.423
a lot of countries are
phasing out nuclear.

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:30.290
I shouldn't be able to stand here.

00:04:30.290 --> 00:04:32.740
This reactor should've
run for about 35 years,

00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:35.090
and now it'll be almost
finished being decommissioned.

00:04:35.090 --> 00:04:37.400
There'd be hardly anything left.

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:40.210
Instead, we got a training centre,
a filming location,

00:04:40.210 --> 00:04:43.460
and, yeah, a very expensive
occasional tourist attraction.

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:47.100
But perhaps most importantly,
we got a museum.

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:49.400
Because this reactor doesn't
have to be decommissioned.

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:50.890
It's the only one on the planet

00:04:50.890 --> 00:04:55.160
where this 1970s dream of the
atomic age can be preserved,

00:04:55.160 --> 00:04:56.943
if only by accident.

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:01.980
Many, many thanks to Stefan Zach
and all the team at EVN

00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:05.050
who gave us so much access
to this incredible place.

00:05:05.050 --> 00:05:07.930
You can find out more about them
at the link in the description.

